Aya Ishihara, Ph.D.

The University of Texas at Austin, 2004

Supervisors: Gerald W. Hoffmann

C. Fred Moore

The first measurements of surviving correlation structures in the final-state charged hadron distributions produced in collisions of gold nuclei at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}\) = 130 GeV are presented. An abundance of correlation structures reflecting relativistic heavy ion collision dynamics is observed over a large momentum acceptance, transverse momentum range \(0.15\leq\eta\leq 2.0\) GeV/c, pseudorapidity range \(-1.3\leq\eta\leq 1.3\) and full azimuth angle \(-\pi\leq\phi\leq\pi\) among primary hadrons measured by the STAR detector at the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC). Correlation structures are measured by means of two-particle number-density ratios in transverse and axial momentum spaces. Isoscalar and isovector correlations are studied separately by examining pair-charge dependent (CD) and independent (CI) correlation components. Observed correlation structures are consistent with the picture that particle production from color-string fragmentation and fragmentation of partons from initial state partonscattering in p-p collisions is strongly modified by a dissipative color medium created in central heavy ion collisions.

