## Chapter 1 Introduction

The properties of matter which we see even in everyday life have provided us important information about how our world is constructed. However, studies also showed us that our knowledge about matter is quite limited by conditions which can exist on the Earth. The heavy ion (HI) collision experiment described in this dissertation aims to explore the equation of state of matter at extreme conditions which people have never experienced but strongly believe to exist abundantly in the universe, such as in cores of high-density stars. The properties of matter in such extreme condition are not simply predictable by extrapolating our limited knowledge of ordinary matter, but require different theoretical and experimental treatments. The relativistic heavy ion collision, the high energy collision of large nuclei which drives matter to an extreme condition, is quite different from general experiments which propose to explore the properties of matter, especially in the sense that the created system is not stable, but dynamically evolving and decaying into ordinary matter before it is observed.

For this reason we do not have control of the basic statistical parameters of the matter, such as volume, pressure or temperature, nor control on timefor the system to be independent of initial conditions. Consequently, in this experiment simultaneous consistent measurements of many observables from different directions are required to get a picture of heavy ion collision dynamics and created matter. None of observables can provide a satisfactory picture of the system by itself, and the difficulty in measuring multiple observables in a consistent way has caused this heavy ion experiment to be large international efforts.

Among the various approaches to describe heavy ion collision dynamics and the properties of matter in extreme condition, analysis of fluctuations and correlations reveals the dynamics which exceed statistical expectation models. Correlations among particles indicate dynamical sources of particle production. Source identification and characterization of correlation structure in heavy ion collisions provide information about particle production mechanisms and transport (diffusion) properties of the system which are closely related to the description of the equilibration process. The tool to achieve this purpose must be very precise and generally multi-dimensional, as its connectivity to other observables is critical in heavy ion physics.

My work described in this dissertation intends to provide a quantitative description of correlation structure which accesses the dynamics of the collision system or the transport properties of matter created in the relativistic heavy ion collisions in a general way.

In the following section, we review why the physics associated with the matter created in heavy ion collisions is expected be different from that of ordinary matter and the expected scenario of heavy ion collision evolution.

### Heavy Ion Collisions and QCD Expectation

The interaction among color-charged particles such as quarks and gluons, called the strong interaction, has been studied within the framework of Quantum Chromodynamics (QCD) [1]. At large momentum transfer QCD predictions have been successfully tested in many experiments [2, 3]. QCD was introduced as an analogue of Quantum Electrodynamics (QED). As a consequence of different transformation properties of the fields (Abelian to non-Abelian) the symmetries associated with QCD describe the interaction of color-charged quarks by the exchanges of gluons which also carry color charges, while the electromagnetic interaction is described by exchanges of chargeless photons. This is a fundamental difference between the theories.

There are two characteristic properties of the strong interaction most distinguishable from other interactions. One, called _asymptotic freedom_ implies that current quarks can move almost freely within a typical hadron radius, which led to the success of bag models where the properties of hadrons are described by confined quarks moving freely within a hadron bag [4, 5]. The other is _quark confinement_, where the interaction among quarks becomes so strong at large distance that quarks are confined within hadrons, consequences of which are the large QCD coupling constant at large distance and the fact that we have never observed free quarks outside hadron radii. Hadrons are particles formed by combining constituent colored quarks in colorless combinations, with a cloud of virtual quarks and gluons which are together called partons. These features of QCD interaction, that all the quarks observed in nature are bound within hadrons or that the long-range interaction can only be treated non-perturbatively because of the large coupling constant, make it difficult to study the nature of this fundamental interaction at distances larger than the hadron radius (\(\sim\) 1-5 fm).

Relativistic heavy ion collisions are proposed to study the long-range nature of the strong interaction experimentally by creating an extreme condition of very high temperature and energy-density. The QCD interaction in such extreme thermodynamic conditions is predicted to be so much weaker than that in cold nuclear matter, such as in our ordinarily world, that bound quarks are released and may obtain new degree of freedom [6, 7]. This new state of matter is called the deconfined phase, or quark-gluon plasma (QGP). The QGP is an idealized thermodynamically equilibriated state of strongly (color force) interacting matter, and the question whether these conditions are satisfied in heavy ion collisions is the subject of this study. Lattice QCD calculations predict that the transition to the deconfined phase is associated with a rapid energy-density increase with temperature and a more gradual increase in pressure indicating the existence of a phase transition at the critical temperature \(T_{c}\) of \(150\sim 170\) MeV at zero net baryon density [8, 9].

What we expect in heavy ion collisions and not in collisions of elementary particles for such a study is formation and development of thermodynamical properties, such as temperature, pressure, entropy, volume and energy density in at least local equilibrium. The highest temperatures that have been created in collisions of gold nuclei accelerated by the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider (RHIC) at Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL) are expected to be above the lattice prediction values of critical temperature/energy-density. Heavy ion collisions represent an extraordinary opportunity to explore the question of phase transitions and QCD interactions. The experimental observation of properties of matter in the extremes of temperature and pressure will significantly extend our understanding of one of the most fundamental interactions and the equation of state of nuclear matter.

RHIC collisions provide us an opportunity to study QCD at finite temperature and very high energy density. However, in an actual heavy ion collision which evolves as a dynamical system, the sufficient condition for matter to exist as a QGP may only be partially achieved. In this dissertation, we establish a general analysis method for measurement of correlations at large momentum scale by which detailed studies of the created dynamical system become possible.

### 2 Space-Time Evolution of Collisions

The system around the collision point induced by the colliding nuclei is a dynamically evolving system in time and space. It has been recognized that the dynamics of heavy ion collisions are not simple but a complicated mixture of different physical aspects. In Fig 1.1, the expected space-time evolution of the interaction dynamics of heavy ion collisions is shown schematically. Thisfigure shows that space-time evolution of the heavy ion collision is initiated by a scattering of composite nucleons or partons in the colliding nuclei at time \(t_{0}\). Such initial scattering and associated energy transformations should be similar to that observed in elementary collisions such as proton-proton (p-p) collisions. It has been observed in p-p collisions that the total cross-section has elastic and inelastic components. The kinetic energies of colliding nucleons are kept in elastic reactions. In inelastic scattering, a certain amount of the kinetic energy is converted into production of primary particles, particles directly produced from the interaction region and not from decays of short life-time particles. Because the initial colliding beams carry only longitudinal kinetic energies,

Figure 1.1: A schematic view of space-time evolution of collisions

the energy-momentum of particles in the transverse direction is of particular interest and transverse dynamics is expected to be different from that along the beam direction. In general, precise information about equilibration processes is only accessible through transverse variables. This is due to the fact that there is a large collective flow or velocity component dominating in the longitudinal direction, and thermal aspects of the axial momenta of particles is primarily affected.

For this reasons, the vector-momentum of particles is effectively expressed in terms of longitudinal component \(p_{z}\) and transverse component \(p_{t}\) which is the projection of three-vector momentum \(\vec{p}\) onto the transverse plane perpendicular to the beam (z-)axis. The longitudinal component of momentum is expressed in terms of (longitudinal) rapidity variable \(y_{z}\), _i.e._,

\[y_{z}=\frac{1}{2}\ln{(\frac{E+p_{z}}{E-p_{z}})}, \tag{1.1}\]

and the direction of momentum around the beam axis is expressed by an azimuth angle \(\phi\).

The rapidity variable has the advantage of a constant additive property under Lorentz transformation. The transformation from rapidity \(y_{z}\) in the center of mass frame to rapidity \(y_{z1}\) in the frame moving with relative velocity \(v\) in the z-direction can be expressed as \(y_{z1}=y_{z}-\frac{1}{2}\ln{\frac{1+v}{1-v}}\). In high energy experiments, because rapidity requires the measurement of two kinematic variables, mass and \(p_{z}\) of the particle for example, the pseudorapidity variable is often preferred over rapidity because it requires only one kinematic variable, the polar angle \(\theta\) between the beam axis and the particle momentum. Pseudorapidity \(\eta\) is defined as,

\[\eta=-\ln\,(\tan\frac{\theta}{2}). \tag{1.2}\]

When the energy of the particle is large compared to its mass, the pseudorapidity and the rapidity of a particle are approximately the same, \(\eta\simeq y_{z}\).

Phase (a) in Fig. 1.1, the pre-equilibrium phase of the time interval between \(t_{0}\) and \(t_{a}\) and the region near the colliding point, corresponds to the phase in which a series of parton scatterings occurs and the nature of the scatterings is expected to be similar to that in p-p collisions. The parton scattering in the p-p collisions is treated in part by perturbative QCD (pQCD), or QCD associated with the small coupling constant, as a reasonable approximation.

During the period of initial parton scattering, a certain amount of energy is deposited into a small interaction volume. The rapidity distribution of primary particles produced in p-p collision experiments [10] show an approximate trapezoidal distribution on longitudinal rapidity \(y_{z}\). The leading baryons, which represent most of the net baryon number initially carried by the colliding nuclei, are observed in the large rapidity region in the center of mass frame. These observations are consistent with the picture that each nucleon in the beam may experience several scatterings as it encounters other nucleons or partons. From experimental observations in p-p collisions, the picture that some degree of symmetry along the beam axis (boost invariance) in the cylindrical region at mid-rapidity and near the beam axis may exist and the longitudinal expansion of such a region with respect to the beam axis has been proposed [11]. However, the particle production mechanism near mid-rapidity is considered to be a mixture of soft and hard processes, and the Lorentz invariance picture may only be applicable to the soft particles.

A particle produced via soft process is called a soft particle. The soft process belongs to QCD of small momentum transfer, and this non-perturbative QCD process with a large coupling constant can only be studied phenomenologically. A hard process involve QCD with large momentum transfers, for which the coupling constant is small and pQCD may be applicable [12, 13]. In consequence, the average hard-particle transverse momentum is larger than that for soft particles. The different transferred-momentum regions of particle production correspond to different particle distributions and subsequent histories of particles. Particle transverse-momentum spectra are dependent on soft-to-hard particle ratios, and in general the regions of spectra in which soft or hard particles are dominated are described by power-law distributions with different slope parameters. Although those distribution of particles of different origins are not strictly distinguishable by the single-particle distribution and the high transverse momentum in phase (a), the early era of collision, does not imply high-\(p_{t}\) particles at later times, it is observed from high energy p-p collisions that hard particles dominant for \(p_{t}\geq 1\) GeV/c, and soft particles dominate for relatively low transverse momentum, typically \(\leq\)1 GeV/c. Because the particle distribution falls exponentially with transverse momentum, more than 80 % of particles are included in the soft-momentumregion in Au-Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=130\) GeV.

With a sufficient number of initial nucleon and parton multiple scatterings, a certain degree of local thermalization is expected to be achieved at phase (b) of Fig. 1.1. But the degree of achieved equilibrium in this phase is still an unrevealed quantity, and an important topic of study in heavy ion physics. If the energy density achieved in this phase is high enough, parton-parton scatterings of mean free path shorter than that for nucleon degrees of freedom are considered to further accelerate the equilibration process. This expectation motivated the study of matter in extreme condition using heavy ion collisions. The temperature achieved in this equilibrium phase is expected to be higher than the temperature necessary to deconfine quarks, and the phase transition to a QGP may occur in this phase. A large pressure and associated expansion are expected with such a high energy density and matter density.

As the incident nuclear pancakes pass through and fly away from the collision point and the distance between them exceeds their diameter, created high energy-density matter expands transversely and axially, and eventually the energy density crosses the QCD phase boundary again. Quarks are reconfined within hadrons or hadrons are fragmented from the color field in phase (c) of Fig. 1.1. As the hadronization process probably does not occur all at once, some parts in space and time of this phase may exist as mixtures of hadrons and color-deconfined matter. Hadron density is still high in phase (c) and it is considered to be a strongly-interacting hadron gas. As the hadrons still undergo radial expansion, the scattering among hadrons ceasesat the kinetic freeze-out (decoupling) time \(t_{la\,st}\). The momentum distribution of particles actually measured by our detectors is considered to be unchanged from freeze-out time, excepting small distortions by the final state (long-range Coulomb and nuclear) interaction among particles between kinetic freeze-out and the time of observation, which are the subjects of systematic corrections.

Although our interest is mostly in the properties of QCD interaction in phase (b), all effects from interactions in phase (a), (b), (c) are contributing to the observed quantities, and some are expected to dilute the signals created in the deconfined phase. The investigation of statistical properties of produced strongly-interacting matter and its dynamical evolution with measured quantities observed at the final state is one of the most challenging tasks in the heavy ion program. There has been a number of possible signatures proposed to study the dynamics of heavy ion collision which are sensitive to the space-time region of possible QGP formation. Each quantity is constructed to reflect some properties of the inter-phase [14], and measured quantities are compared with expected values contributed from (a) and from the hadronic final state, which are obtained from hadron-hadron and hadron-nucleus collision experiments. By comparing these results of elementary interactions with heavy ion collisions, we indirectly investigate the interaction in the space-time region of (b) and (c).

Because of this indirect observational nature of heavy ion collisions, the expectations that we derived from elementary collisions play a major role in the relativistic heavy ion experiments. In this study, results are compared with correlation structure observed in p-p collisions and these elementary collisions have exhibited many interesting correlation structures. In p-p collisions, soft particle production was phenomenologically described in terms of a color-string parallel to the beam axis with partons at each end of the string. As the string is stretched along the axis, it fragments to produces other partons at each end of the fragments. The high transverse momentum part of p-p collision spectra are well matched to the description that high-\(p_{t}\) partons resulted from hard parton-parton scattering are fragmented into a cluster of particle in which particles are correlated in momentum. Those pictures of particle production are obtained from correlation structures which result from local conservation of momentum and/or charges. How these particle experiences different histories in Au-Au collisions and carry correlation information to the final state is an important study which may lead to a better understanding of particle production in heavy ion collisions.

Hard particles were expected to'remember' the early stages of the collision more then soft particles but we find that soft particles also remember the earlier phase, which is then to be accessible from final state correlations. Because soft particles mainly fragment from the deconfined color medium, soft-particle correlations are expected to strongly reflect the properties of the color medium.

### Fluctuation and Correlation Analysis

The dynamics of heavy ion collisions produce correlations among particles; thus equivalently the correlation measurements tell us about heavy ion dynamics, and correlations are sometime measured as a fluctuation beyond statistical expectation (non-statistical fluctuation). The _event-by-event_ analysis of global fluctuations has been developed as a precise tool in the search for non-statistical fluctuations of event-wise quantities [15]. Fluctuations in the final state may be related to the statistical properties of the hot matter at the freeze-out time; examples of such connections are shown as the relation of fluctuation of the slope parameter of transverse momentum to temperature fluctuations [16, 17, 18] and thus the heat capacity [19], multiplicity and entropy [20, 21] or the hadronic matter compressibility [22], and particle species fluctuations such as the fluctuations of kaon-to-pion number ratio or positive-to-negative charged-particle ratios may gives information about earlier times [23, 24, 25]. Possible observation of critical fluctuations associated with the phase transition would give direct information about the order of the transition and the effective degrees of freedom in the earlier phase [26].

However, fluctuation analysis in a fixed acceptance cannot determine the mechanism of the fluctuation production by itself but requires additional work to track down the sources of fluctuations. As correlation structure corresponds to the source of dynamical fluctuation, a careful study of correlations in a general way and form relatable to fluctuation analysis will open up new paths to understanding fluctuations.

The two-particle correlation analysis presented in this dissertation serves such a role and gives further understanding on underlying correlation and particle production mechanisms in relativistic heavy ion collisions, thus the regime of non-perturbative QCD.

### Overview

The purpose of the study described in this dissertations is the qualitative evaluation in a model independent way of surviving correlation structure in the hadronic final state produced in ultra relativistic heavy ion collisions. The sampled collisions used in this analysis are created by RHIC and recorded with the STAR detector. In Chapter 2, an overview of the collider and experimental setup used for the first-year data taking is presented. The appropriate forms of correlation analysis for a system which involves complex dynamical evolution is considered in Chapter 3. The relation between correlation analysis and the study of fluctuations of the event-by-event global observables such as event-wise average \(p_{t}\) is also studied. Detailed particle production mechanisms in heavy ion collisions are investigated by comparison of the obtained correlation structure with the known correlation context from elementary collisions, such as cluster/string fragmentation or mini-jets. In Chapter 4, physics involved in the two-particle analysis and expected correlation structure from elementary collisions are discussed. Data are selected so as to exclude effects from online and offline systematics. In Chapter 5 detailed event, track and particle pair selections are described. Finally, analytic results for transverse and axial momentum spaces are presented and discussed in Chapters 6 - 8.

## Chapter 2 Experiment

The data analysis presented in this dissertation is based on event samples of Au-Au collisions at center of mass energy of 130 GeV per nucleon pair (\(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=130\) GeV) produced by the Relativistic Heavy-Ion Collider (RHIC) at the Brookhaven National Laboratory (BNL) and measured by the Solenoidal Tracker at RHIC (STAR) detector during Summer of year 2000, the first-year RHIC run.

RHIC is designed to accelerate various kinds of ions and collide them at the top energy ranging from 200 GeV to 500 GeV depending on the ion species [27]. The heaviest nuclei to be accelerated is gold nuclei (atomic mass 197) which can be accelerated up to \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=200\) GeV. While the energy achieved during the first-year run was not full designed energy, the energy increase from past experiments is very large. Past, present and future heavy-ion accelerators and their achieved (planned) energies are listed in Table 2.1. Although the ion species flexibility of the RHIC is notable and crucial for the other important physics purposes, in the following only the RHIC operation in the case with gold ions is described.

### RHIC Facility

The main components of the RHIC are two intersecting rings of superconducting synchrotrons. RHIC has the capability to make collisions at six intersection positions in its 3,843 m circumference. The important components of the synchrotron rings are 1760 superconducting magnets kept at a temperature of \(<4.6\) K to bend and control tracks of ion beams, and 4 rf cavities. The basic RHIC parameters are listed in Table 2.2.

