## Chapter 3 Two-particle Correlation Analysis Methods

This study is the first two-particle correlation analysis of all three momentum components for particles produced at the RHIC energy. In this chapter, we consider the appropriate forms of correlation analysis suitable for such study, and of the most general forms because correlation mechanisms in heavy ion collisions at this energy are still unknown. After sufficient general study, identification of correlations which belong to different physical mechanisms and specific correlation study focusing on each mechanism may become possible. We elicit correlation contents from two-particle density \(\rho_{2}\) which contains all two-particle correlations. The extraction of the interesting correlation contents is performed by comparing \(\rho_{2}\) and a two-particle reference density distribution. In the last section we show the connection between correlation analysis and fluctuation analysis, and thus demonstrate that this work reveals the dynamical origins of excess event-by-event fluctuations.

### 3.1 Two-particle Densities

Two-particle density \(\rho_{2}\left(p_{t1},\eta_{1},\phi_{1},p_{2},\eta_{2},\phi_{2}\right)\) is the pair-number distribution normalized by dividing by the total number of pairs in the two-particlemomentum space and is obtained by counting numbers of pairs in each of \(N_{bin,1}\cdot N_{bin,2}\) bins of size \(\delta p_{1}\cdot\delta p_{2}\) defined in the 6-dimensional two-particle momentum space. The size of 6-dimensional two-particle momentum space under study is written as a product of 3-dimensional single-particle momentum acceptances \(\Delta p_{1}\cdot\Delta p_{2},\) where \(\Delta p\equiv(\Delta p_{ti},\Delta\eta_{i},\Delta\phi_{i})\) indicates the momentum acceptance of the \(i_{th}\) particle in a pair, defined by the acceptance of transverse momentum \(\Delta p_{ti},\) pseudorapidity \(\Delta\eta_{i}\) and azimuth angle \(\Delta\phi_{i}.\)

A two-particle density can be formed for any observable quantity. In this study, two-particle number densities \(\rho_{2}\) are formed for pairs with like-sign (LS) and unlike-sign (US) charge-combinations, motivated by the correlation structures observed in p-p collisions. The LS and US two-particle densities are constructed with the assumption of charge symmetry of two-particle distributions: _i.e._, \(n_{2}^{++}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})\simeq n_{2}^{--}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})\) and \(n_{2}^{+-}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})\simeq n_{2}^{-+}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}),\) where \(n_{2}^{ab}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})\) denotes the number distribution of pairs in which one particle has momentum \(\vec{p_{1}}\) with its charge sign \(a\) and the other has \(\vec{p_{2}}\) and \(b.\) The LS and US two-particle density distributions are written as,

\[\rho_{2}^{LS}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})=\frac{n_{2}^{\frac{++}{+}}(\vec{p_{1}}, \vec{p_{2}})+n_{2}^{\overline{+}-}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})}{\int_{\Delta p_{1 }}\int_{\Delta p_{2}}n_{2}^{\frac{++}{+}}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})+n_{2}^{ \overline{-}+}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})\ \ d\vec{p_{1}}\ d\vec{p_{2}}}. \tag{3.1}\]

The total numbers of LS and US pairs including double-counting pairs in an event are simply related to the total number of positive (negative) charged particles \(N^{+(-)}\) in the event by \(\int_{\Delta p_{1}}\int_{\Delta p_{2}}n_{2}^{\pm\pm}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}) d\vec{p_{1}}\ d\vec{p_{2}}=N^{\pm}(N^{\pm}-1)\) for LS and \(\int_{\Delta p_{1}}\int_{\Delta p_{2}}n_{2}^{\pm\mp}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}) d\vec{p_{1}}d\vec{p_{2}}=N^{\pm}\cdot N^{\mp}\) for US before the pair-wise cuts (Sec. 5.3) are applied.

The dimensionality of two-particle distributions can be reduced by integrating over variables other than those of interest, for visualization and further analysis. For example, the two-particle transverse-momentum distribution is obtained by integrating the 6-dimensional function Eq. 3.1 over two-particle axial momenta (\(\eta_{1}\),\(\eta_{2}\),\(\phi_{1}\),\(\phi_{2}\)) as,

\[\rho_{2}\left(p_{1},\,p_{t2}\right)=\int_{\Delta\eta_{1}}\int_{\Delta\eta_{2}} \int_{\Delta\phi_{1}}\int_{\Delta\phi_{2}}\rho_{2}\left(\vec{p_{1}},\,\vec{p_{ 2}}\right)d\eta_{1}d\eta_{2}d\phi_{1}d\phi_{2}\,. \tag{3.2}\]

To obtain the correlation contents of interest, the two-particle distributions are compared with reference distributions which include all correlations except those under study, by taking differences or ratios. Experimentally, the mixed-pair distribution \(\rho_{2,mix}\) serves as such a reference. Mixed pairs are pairs whose constituent particles belong to different events selected to have the same single-particle distribution as the distribution of pairs from the same events but which do not contain two-particle correlations. The details of such mixed-pair construction are discussed in Sec. 5.5.

The correlation function \(C_{2}\) is defined as a difference between two-particle object distribution \(\rho_{2}\) and reference distribution \(\rho_{2,mix}\),

\[C_{2}\left(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\right)\equiv\rho_{2}\left(\vec{p_{1}},\vec {p_{2}}\right)-\rho_{2,mix}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}). \tag{3.3}\]

The comparison may also be obtained by taking ratios instead of differences. Because both numerator and denominator have the same single-particle efficiency distributions, by taking the ratios, effects from the detector acceptance and efficiency distribution cancel at the single-particle level. Thus, we can study the two-particle correlation avoiding difficulties arising from the correction of single-particle efficiency changes in two-particle distributions, which would require a model of the associated two-particle correlations. For this reason, the _two-particle density ratio_\(R(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})\) is measured in this study, defined by

\[R(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}) \equiv \frac{\rho_{2\,,\,{\it sib}}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})}{\rho_{2\,, \,{\it mix}}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})} \tag{3.4}\] \[= \frac{C_{2}\left(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\right)}{\rho_{2\,,\,{\it mix }}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}})}+1,\]

where \(\rho_{2,\,{\it sib}}\) denotes the two-particle density of same-event pairs (sibling-pairs).

In terms of \(R\), correlations are expressed as deviations from 1, and thus the deviation quantity \(R\)**-**1 is often plotted. As the construction procedure of two-particle density ratios includes binning of continuous momentum variables, what the momentum variables indicate in the following is the central value of the momentum bin with indices \(i,j\), _i.e._\(p_{i}\) indicates the central value of the i\({}_{th}\) momentum bin, and \(R_{ij}\) indicates the two-particle density ratio in momentum bin of \(p_{i}\) and \(p_{j}\), although indices are normally omitted if there is no confusions.

### Autocorrelations and Joint Autocorrelations

The _autocorrelation function_ is generally defined as the cross-correlation of a signal at \(x\) with the signal itself at different points \(x+x_{\Delta}\)[36, 37] which can be expressed as one-dimensional correlation histograms on pair difference variables. This form is useful when correlation structures have small dependence on sum variables and are then approximately expressed as a function of difference variables only.

In this analysis, the autocorrelation function \(A(p_{\Delta})\) of pair momentum difference variables (\(p_{t\Delta}\equiv p_{t1}-p_{t2}\), \(\eta_{\Delta}\equiv\eta_{1}-\eta_{2}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\equiv\phi_{1}-\phi_{2}\)) is constructed from a two-particle density ratio by taking an average over pair sum variables (\(p_{\Sigma}\equiv p_{t1}+p_{t2}\), \(\eta_{\Sigma}\equiv\eta_{1}+\eta_{2}\), \(\phi_{\Sigma}\equiv\phi_{1}+\phi_{2}\)) or in other words the average of slices of the two-particle density ratio at different pair-sum variables. Thus the autocorrelation function is related to the two-particle density ratio \(R\) by

\[A\left(p_{\Delta,\,j}\right)\equiv\frac{1}{N_{bin,\,\Sigma}}\sum_{i\,=1}^{N_{ bin,\,\Sigma}}\left[R(p_{\Sigma,i},\,p_{\Delta,j})-1\right], \tag{3.5}\]

where \(N_{bin,\,\Sigma}\) is the number of bins along constant \(p_{\Delta,\,j}\) diagonals. If the two-particle density ratio has small dependence on the sum variables, the dimension

