### Multiplicity Dependence of Model Correlations

Inclusive correlation functions obtained from the different multiplicity classes are compared in a search of the correlation production/propagation mechanisms and their modifications with centrality variation of heavy ion collisions. For this purpose any trivial multiplicity dependencies of correlation function should be understood. Appropriate normalizations for the comparison of two-particle correlations from different multiplicity classes are considered in this section.

The correlation function defined in Eq. 3.3 reflects the ratio of pairs correlated within events, which can be observed by comparing correlation functions of given width and form in which all the pairs are correlated (\(C_{2}\) ) and where a fraction \(f\) of pairs are correlated (\(C_{2}^{f}\)). In the latter case, the two-particle density \(\rho_{2}^{f}\) is written as the sum of the fraction \(f\) pairs which are different from the reference and the fraction (\(1-f\) ) of reference (uncorrelated)pairs. Thus,

\[C_{2}^{f}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\,) = \rho_{2}^{f}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\,)-\rho_{2,mi\,x}(\vec{p_{1}} \,,\vec{p_{2}}\,) \tag{3.7}\] \[= f\rho_{2}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\,)+(1-f)\rho_{2,mi\,x}(\vec{p_{ 1}},\vec{p_{2}}\,)-\rho_{2,mix}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\,)\] \[= f\,C_{2}(\vec{p_{1}},\vec{p_{2}}\,),\]

we see that the amplitude of the correlation function is proportional to \(f\) corresponding to dilution by uncorrelated pairs.

However, considering the nature of correlations, it is natural to scale with the fraction of _particles_ correlated in events rather than the fraction of correlated pairs, \(i.e.\) the amplitude should be proportional to the number of particles correlated with a test particle. As seen from the relation between fluctuation measurements and two-particle correlations (Eq. 3.16), the normalization is approximately obtained by multiplying the correlation function by the event multiplicity. Thus, the normalized two-particle density ratio assuming approximate factorization of correlation contents is written as \((\overline{N_{j}}-1)\)\((R-1)\).

### Fluctuations and Two-Particle Correlations

A way to examine the role of two-particle correlations in heavy ion collisions is to consider their relation to fluctuations of event-wise averages, namely mean-\(p_{t}\) fluctuations, which provided initial motivation to the study of two-particle correlation in transverse-momentum space.

Event-by-event fluctuation analysis of event-wise \(p_{t}\) average has been recognized as a sensitive tool to study the dynamics of heavy ion collisions[15, 16, 26]. Theoretical models have predicted the behavior of nonstatistical fluctuations associated with the phase transition in heavy ion collisions, including enhancement _and_ reduction of fluctuation due to the presence of critical phenomena associated with the phase transition, event-by-event fluctuation of equilibration process and the memory effect from the phase with partonic degrees of freedom in final state hadrons due to rapid expansion of the system [23, 24]. Given these possibilities of interesting physics, the collision energy/ion species and centrality (multiplicity) dependence of fluctuations become most important, with the expectation of observing irregular enhancement or reduction of fluctuations as functions of collision properties, and the search for sources of such non-statistical fluctuations.

There are several proposed observables to look for multiplicity or participant number dependencies of the dynamically-induced fluctuations [38, 39, 40]. Variable \(\Delta\sigma_{p_{t}}^{2}\) was constructed as a \(p_{t}\) variance-difference measure to have the least contribution from multiplicity fluctuations in a set of events. It is suitable for careful study of event ensembles in which different numbers of particles are involved. For this reason \(\Delta\sigma_{p_{t}}^{2}\) has been measured in the STAR experiment [41].

\(\Delta\sigma_{p_{t}}^{2}\) measures \(<p_{t}>\) variance beyond the statistically independent reference inferred from the central limit theorem (CLT) [39] and is defined by

\[\Delta\sigma_{p_{t}}^{2}=\overline{N_{j}\sigma_{<p_{t}>,j}^{2}}-\sigma_{\vec{p }_{t}}^{2}, \tag{3.8}\]

where event-wise mean of quantity \(a_{ji}\) of \(i_{th}\) particle over \(j_{th}\) event with multi

