## Chapter 4 Expected Sources of Correlations

There are several expected sources of large-momentum-scale two-particle correlations in relativistic heavy ion collisions. These correlations may appear as modifications of correlations observed in p-p collisions or as specific to heavy ion collisions. Correlation structure in elementary collisions suggests the asymptotic behavior to be expected in very peripheral heavy ion collisions and provides a reference model in search of the characteristic behavior in more central heavy ion collisions. Some of the correlation structure expected to appear in heavy ion two-particle momentum spaces relevant to this study are described in this chapter. The large-momentum-scale two-particle correlation structures we observe in Au-Au collisions are quantitatively described with these models in Chapters 6 - 8.

### 4.1 Temperature Fluctuations in Transverse-Momentum Space

High temperature corresponds to some characteristic energy is considered to be one of the most essential conditions for the existence of a phase transition. Thus, (at least local) temperature equilibrium is assumed in most heavy ion collision models which involve a color-deconfined phase. Because thedegree of achieved equilibrium is not directly observable, the expected degree depends on assumptions made in each model.

In this section, we consider a two-particle transverse-momentum distribution in the generic case that particles are emitted from small spacial regions in local equilibrium but that (local) temperatures may fluctuate from point to point in each collision (local fluctuations) and/or from event to event (global fluctuations). The case that each event is fully equilibrated but the mean temperature varies event by event is treated as a limiting case. The assumed temperature distribution model in this section, with local or global temperature fluctuations, follows a generic Langevin equation and is represented by gamma distribution. In this model, the experimentally observed power-law or the Levy single-particle \(p_{\!\!\!R}\) distribution is derived from the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution by folding with the gamma temperature distribution. The temperature distribution is also represented in a two-particle space by assuming two-point temperature correlation models, and the two-point temperature distribution is then reflected in the two-particle transverse momentum space. Comparison with a hypothetical model of two-particle transverse momentum correlations arising from event local/global temperature fluctuations and experimentally observed data may give insight into the equilibration process in heavy ion collisions, and thus may test the applicability of equilibrium models. It should be noted that for the present study with unidentified charged particles, the 'temperature' discussed in this section includes not only thermal random motion but also contributions from collective motion of particles, both of which may fluctuate locally and/or globally.

The assumption of thermodynamic equilibrium in heavy ion collisions is based on the fact that single-particle distributions are approximately described by Maxwell-Boltzmann (MB) distributions in the soft-\(p_{t}\) region. However, with recent observations of deviations from equilibrium expectations such as nonstatistical fluctuations [41] and mixing of soft and hard particle distributions [43], it is natural to consider that some degree of temperature variation exists in a set of heavy ion collisions at the RHIC energy. Thus, we consider the possible form of temperature fluctuations included in single-particle distributions based on the generic Langevin equation [44] describing stochastic temperature variable T with mean temperature \(\overline{T}\). The equation describes temperature variation of a small selected region next to a large heat bath and is represented by

\[\frac{dT}{dt}=-\frac{T}{\kappa}-\xi(t)T+\frac{\overline{T}}{\kappa}, \tag{4.1}\]

where \(\xi(t)\) is a random gaussian white noise defined by \(<\xi(t)>=0\) and \(<\xi(t)\xi(t+\Delta t)>=2D\delta(\Delta t)\). Coefficient \(D\) is a constant which indicates the amplitude of the random noise and \(\kappa\) is a damping constant. The Langevin eq. (Eq. 4.1) is different from the Langevin equation of Brownian motion in that (1) the noise from random temperature fluctuations, the second term in the right-hand side, is proportional to the temperature itself and (2) there is a constant source term \(\overline{T}/\kappa\).

#### 4.1.1 Single-Point Temperature Distributions

The single-point temperature probability distribution function (pdf) \(\tau\) which satisfies Eq. 4.1 is represented by a gamma distribution \(g\) of inverse-temperature \(\beta=1/T\) as,

\[\tau\left(\beta;\overline{\beta},n\right)=g(\beta;\overline{\beta},n) \equiv \frac{n}{\overline{\beta}\Gamma(n)}\big{(}\frac{n\beta}{\overline {\beta}}\big{)}^{n-1}e^{-n\frac{\beta}{\overline{\beta}}}, \tag{4.2}\]

where \(\overline{\beta}=1/\overline{T}\) denotes the mean inverse-temperature and \(n=1/\kappa D\). \(\tau(\beta)\) denotes the general form of temperature distributions and \(g(\beta)\) denotes a gamma-function model temperature distribution. With the normalization \(\int_{0}^{\infty}g(\beta;\beta_{0},n)d\beta=1\), the relative variance is related to \(n\) by

\[\frac{\sigma_{\beta}^{2}}{\overline{\beta}^{2}}=\frac{<(\beta-\overline{\beta })^{2}>}{\overline{\beta}^{2}}=\frac{1}{n}. \tag{4.3}\]

The finite-width temperature pdf (Eq. 4.2) results in a modification of the inclusive single-particle MB distribution of a static thermal source as,

\[\frac{1}{m_{t}}\frac{dN(m_{t})}{dm_{t}} = B\int_{0}^{\infty}\!\!g(\beta;\overline{\beta},n)\;e^{-\beta m_{ t}}\,d\beta \tag{4.4}\] \[= \frac{B}{(1+\frac{\overline{\beta}}{n})^{n}}=F_{Levy}(m_{t}; \overline{\beta},n).\]

The inclusive single-particle distribution with relative temperature variance \(1/n\) is written as a Levy distribution \(F_{Levy}(m_{t};\overline{\beta},n)\) and the non-zero variance may arise from local and/or event-wise temperature fluctuations. For the case of \(\frac{1}{n}\to 0\), \(g\left(\beta;\overline{\beta},\infty\right)=\delta(\beta-\overline{\beta})\), and the Levy distribution (Eq. 4.4) becomes the MB exponential distribution.

It has been observed that the Levy distribution gives good agreement with the single-particle \(p_{t}\) distribution in the heavy ion collisions [45]. Most of the difference between the MB and data distribution is in general considered to be due to a mixture of collective radial motion of particles and particles originating from semi-hard scattering. However, the fraction of contribution arising from difference sources is still an open question. It relates to an essential question of heavy ion physics, the relation between single-point temperature distribution \(\tau\left(\beta\right)\) and inverse-temperature parameter \(\beta\equiv 1/T\). In the following, given the assumed single-point gamma temperature distribution \(\tau(\beta)=g\left(\beta\right)\), two-point temperature distributions are considered.

#### 4.1.2 Two-Point Temperature Distributions

The concept of temperature distribution is also applicable in a two-particle space. For the simplest case, the _two-point temperature distribution_\(\tau_{2}\) of a statistical reference or a factorized distribution is written as a product of two single-point temperature distributions (Eq. 4.2) as,

\[\tau_{2,stat}(\beta_{1},\beta_{2};\overline{\beta},n)=\tau(\beta_{1}; \overline{\beta},n)\cdot\tau(\beta_{2};\overline{\beta},n). \tag{4.5}\]

The non-factorized two-point temperature distribution \(\tau_{2}\left(\beta_{1},\beta_{2};\overline{\beta},n^{\prime}(\Delta e),n( \Sigma e)\right)\) for a set of events characterized by inclusive mean inverse temperature \(\overline{\beta}\), local-temperature-fluctuation parameter \(n^{\prime}(\Delta e)\) and global-temperature-fluctuation parameter \(n\left(\Sigma e\right)\), may be expressed simply to a single-particle distribution (Eq. 4.2) by two-point temperature distribution of which local inverse-temperature\(\beta_{0}\) weighted by a mean temperature distribution \(\tau(\beta_{0})\) as,

\[\tau_{2}(\beta_{1},\beta_{2};\overline{\beta},\,n^{\prime}(\Delta e),n(\Sigma e)) \tag{4.6}\] \[= \int\tau_{2}\left(\beta_{1},\beta_{2};\beta_{0},n^{\prime}(\Delta e )\right)\cdot\tau\left(\beta_{0};\overline{\beta},n(\Sigma e)\right)\,d\beta_{0},\]

