Color-String Fragmentation -- Charge Ordering and Local Momentum Conservation on Axial-Momentum Space

Two-particle correlations among particles created in elementary collisions have been studied and well described by phenomenological models. Such models serve as references for two-particle correlation structure in heavy ion collisions. They describe the expected asymptotic behavior of two-particle correlations in the most peripheral heavy ion collisions or the form of two-particle correlations in the case that A-A collisions are merely superpositions of N-N collisions.

Two-particle correlations for axial momentum variables (\(\eta\), \(\phi\)) in p-p collisions were studied for like-sign (LS) and unlike-sign (US) charged-pairs as well as charge independent (CI = like-sign + unlike-sign) pairs [46, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52]. The CI two-particle correlations in light-nucleus collisions [53] or in heavy ion collisions at lower energies [54] also exhibited similar correlation structures to those in p-p collisions.

The most prominent feature of the two-particle correlation structure observed in p-p collisions is the large-momentum-scale correlation (LSC) of (pseudo-)rapidity differences as shown in the form of two-particle density ratio \(R=\frac{\rho(y_{1},y_{2})}{\rho_{stat}(y_{1},y_{2})}-1\) (described in Sec. 3.1) for three different collision energies in Fig. 4.1. The LSC are approximately described as functions of rapidity difference \(y_{\Delta}\equiv y_{1}-y_{2}\). The amplitude and shape of the correlations have small dependence on the collision energy, except that the shape is more extended along the sum diagonal at higher energies. Separate correlation structures for LS (++ and --) and US (+- and -+) charge-pair combinations are shown in Fig. 4.2. LSC correlations are clearly observed for US pairs; small-momentum-scale correlations (SSC) with correlation length shorter than for

