### 4.3 Anisotropic Flow and Charge-Independent Axial-Momentum Correlations

In heavy ion collisions the incident nuclei have relatively large cross sections compare to elementary particles and there is a variation of the impact parameter \(\vec{b}\) defined by the relative transverse positions of the colliding nuclei (Fig. 4.4). In events with non-zero impact parameter (non-central events), it has been observed that anisotropy of the initial collision geometry leads to event-by-event anisotropic final-state particle distributions on azimuth angle \(\phi\). The anisotropic particle distribution is often parameterized by Fourier coefficients \(v_{n}\) of the relative azimuth angle with respect to the reaction-plane angle \(\Psi\) defined by the vector angle of impact parameter \(\vec{b}\), as shown in Fig. 4.4. The single particle azimuthal distribution is then expressed as,

\[\rho(\phi;\,\Psi)\propto 1+\sum_{n\,=1}^{\infty}2v_{n}\,cos(n\,(\phi-\Psi)). \tag{4.19}\]

