### Mini-jet Production and Charge-Independent Axial-Momentum Correlations

Particle production associated with parton scattering with large momentum-transfer (high-\(p_{t}\) hard-process) has been observed in element any collisions. The cross-sections and high-\(p_{t}\) part of single-particle spectra are described by models based on perturbative QCD (pQCD) [12, 13, 2]. In Au-Au collisions partons, dynamical constituents of nucleons in incident nucleon beams, experience initial hard-scatterings which are expected to be similar to those in elementary collisions and should also produce high-\(p_{t}\) scattered partons. Each high-\(p_{t}\) parton then fragments to a cluster of particles, with average momentum expected to be higher than that of soft particles (string fragments). Correlated hadron groups (jets) produced in the fragmentation of _minimum-bias_ partons with no high-\(p_{t}\) trigger imposed are called here minijets.

Because the momentum of parent partons is carried by the daughter minijet hadrons [62], particles in a minijet have a strong angular correlation with the jet thrust-axis, which is the momentum direction of the scattered parton, as well as a correlation between the parent parton momentum and the total momentum of particles in the minijet. Thus, it can be expected that minijets display characteristic angular and \(p_{t}\) correlation structure in two-particle correlation spaces.

Although in this work the correlation of particles with \(p_{t}<2\) GeV/c is studied, a significant contribution from hard-process particles (minijets), is observed with axial and transverse momentum correlation measurements. Observations of the suppression of single-particle hadron-spectra at high-\(p_{t}\) (\(p_{t}>2\) GeV/c) [63, 64] and the disappearance of back-to-back jets [43] are indicative of correlation modifications in central heavy ion collisions. Such modification may be resulted from a dense medium created in heavy ion collisions which may reduce parton transverse momentum [65, 66, 67] and modify minijet transverse momentum distributions, or may affect parton fragmentation processes. Minijets may possibly be distinguished by their characteristic two-particle correlation structure. Current study of correlation structure and centrality dependence reveal particle production mechanisms and detailed information about the dense medium created in heavy ion collisions.

## Chapter 5 Analysis Details

The event samples of Au-Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s\,_{NN}}=130\) GeV obtained with two sets of trigger settings may include some artificial effects due to a limit of hardware and offline softwares. In this chapter, selections of events, track-wise and pair-wise cuts applied to data to reduce the effects not relevant to current study are described. These selections are applied before two-particle number-density distributions are constructed.

### 5.1 Event Selections

Stored TPC ADC (Analog to Digital Converter) values, several millions of numbers per event are transformed into physically meaningful quantities by the STAR offline-reconstruction software. Three of the most basic tasks of the event-reconstruction software are the _hit finding_ by which three-dimensional coordinates of hit positions where charged particles crossed in the TPC are obtained, the _tracking of the reconstructed hit positions_ with the best knowledge of calibration parameters and the _collision vertex finding_. A reconstructed track provides vector-momentum and charge-sign of the particle from shape of the track vend in the uniform magnetic field and loosing some of its kineticenergy. Collision-vertex position is determined by extrapolating tracks toward the beam line and a set of tracks associated with the collision-vertex characterizes the event. The vertex positions were varied event by event over TPC length \(z=\pm 2\) m along the beam-axis during the first-year physics run. When collision points are largely offset from the center of TPC, because of the finite size of the TPC, not enough hits are reconstructed to perform precise tracking in both positive and negative pseudorapidity regions. For this reason events with vertex positions in the range \(-75\) cm \(<V_{z}<+75\) cm along the beam axis and \(-3\) cm \(<V_{x,y}<+3\) cm in x-y (transverse) direction are selected in this analysis (Fig. 5.1 (a)).

