### Track Selections

Tracks in the transverse momentum range \(0.15<p_{t}<2.0\) GeV /c are selected in this analysis. The upper \(p_{t}\) limit matches to the selection used in the first single-particle spectra analysis by the STAR collaboration [68]. This selection allows us to apply the study on the single-particle background contribution and tracking efficiency estimation for the spectra analysis directly to this study. In the spectra analysis which requires improved momentum resolution, the upper \(p_{t}\) limit is set by the resolution condition, because the resolution is determined from track helix curvatures and the curvatures are less sensitive for high \(p_{t}\) particles. The lower limit is set to avoid the large contributions from electron backgrounds from photon conversions which exponentially increase with decreasing \(p_{t}\)[69].

The reconstruction efficiency variations associated with vertex-position distribution limit on pseudorapidity range of tracks by -1.3 \(<\eta<\) 1.3. The right panel in Fig. 5.1 shows the pseudorapidity distributions for events with different vertex z-positions. The event-wise efficiency changes with different vertex positions require the construction of mixed-pair reference from events with close vertex positions (Sec. 5.5). The contribution from short-lived particles which decay within several cm from the reconstructed collision vertexes such as \(\Lambda\) and \(K^{0}_{s}\) are reduced by choosing tracks of which distance to closest approach (DCA) to the collision vertex is less than 3 cm. Tracks of bad tracking qualities for example a track which has a large number of fit iterations to obtain a converged value or has many outlier removal and eliminated points,are rejected. In addition to the requirement which requests at least 10 hit points for the tracking of each particle, the number of hit points is requested to be larger than the half of the total possible number of reconstruction points for the track. This conditions are applied for tracking accuracy and to avoid the situation that a true particle trajectory is segmented into two or more pieces and each segment is reconstructed as a track.

### 5.3 Pair Selections

Pair-wise mis-reconstruction effect results in different two-particle reconstruction efficiency distributions for sibling pairs in which both particles are from the same event and for mixed pairs in which particles are from different events. Thus, when sibling-to-mixed pair ratio is taken, the effect is not canceled. This effect resembles as (anti-)correlations in two-particle space and pair-wise correction is required to eliminate them. The basic correction procedure for the pair-tracking effects is to apply the same pair-wise cuts on both sibling-pair and mixed-pair distributions, by which sibling- and mixed-pair efficiency distributions are corrected to have the same distributions. For this reason, study of sufficient condition which excludes the region of momentum space in which pair efficiencies differs for sibling- and mixed-pairs but still retains sufficient number of pairs is an important issue in this analysis.

Geometrical conditions which lead to incorrect reconstructions can be categorized into two by appearance of effects in two-particle space. One is _track-splitting_ which is caused by one particle reconstructed as two or more tracks, appears as positive correlation on two-particle space, and the other is _pair-merging_ which is caused by two or more tracks reconstructed as one track, resembles negative correlation.

Track-splittings are removed by the track-wise requirement that tracks should have the number of hit points used for tracking larger than the half of the total number of possible reconstruction points for the track (hit-ratio cut). By this hit-ratio cut, when a single particle trajectory is reconstructed as more than one track and none of the pieces have greater than 50% of the estimated maximum number of possible hits, then all segments are lost and then this loss is another form of single-particle tracking inefficiency and cancels when sibling- to mixed-pair ratio is taken. However, there is two-track inefficiency aspects, if the loss is not randomly occurred but induced with some pair-wise conditions. The effect induced by the track-splitting are studied based on track separations in TPC active volume. Pair separations are calculated separately at the inner radius of the TPC (\(r=50\,\mathrm{cm}\)) \(d_{t,z}^{in}\), at between the inner and outer pad-sectors (\(r=127\,\mathrm{cm}\)) \(d_{t,z}^{mid}\) and at the TPC outer radius (\(r=200\,\mathrm{cm}\)) \(d_{t,z}^{out}\), where the indices \(t\) and \(z\) indicate the projected distances onto transverse and z directions respectively. The separations are calculated as linear distances between tracks at different radii and is illustrated in the Fig. 5.2.

In Fig. 5.4, pair-ratio distributions of separation at mid-TPC radius for only positive azimuth angle difference pairs are shown. Pairs with positive (blue points) and negative (red points) transverse-momentum differences for each charge-pair combinations are independently plotted and the differ ence in the distributions are clearly observed. This observation indicates that track-splitting are induced by _pair-crossing_ near the mid-TPC radius as illustrated Fig. 5.3, because only pairs which can have pair-crossing geometries as illustrated in left panels (a) and (b) are suffered from the negative peak and pairs which are free from pair-crossing geometries have positive peak which is consistent with the Bose-Einstein correlation expectation for LS pairs. After the hit-ratio cut, the negative correlation induced by pair-crossing are distinguished from that induced by pair-merging by contrasting their azimuth angle difference and \(p_{t}\) difference dependencies.

