### Charge Dependent (CD) Correlations

The Charge Dependent (CD) two-particle density ratio is constructed by subtracting unlike-sign (US) two-particle density ratio from like-sign (LS) distribution (\(R^{CD}=R^{LS}-R^{US}\)). In Fig. 6.13, centrality dependence of CD two-particle density ratios as function of \(x\,(p_{t})\) after normalization by the mean multiplicity in each centrality bin are presented. The small-momentum-scale correlations are eliminated according to Sec. 5.7.

The large-momentum-scale correlation structure on the CD two-particle transverse momentum spaces includes a concave downward shape along \(x_{\Sigma}\) diagonal observed for all centrality classes. With the observation of small dependence on \(x_{\Delta}\), the dimension of the data can be reduced by projecting the two-dimensional space onto \(x_{\Sigma}\) diagonal axis by taking average of bin contents along difference variable direction without information loss.

Obtained best-fit parameters are listed in Table 6.5. A small centrality dependence of amplitude parameter is observed in the CD transverse two-particle correlation consistent with the charge dependent event-by-event \(<p_{t}>\) fluctuation study (Sec. 3.5). The CD structure at the large \(x_{\Sigma}\) is suggestive of charge configuration in the high-\(p_{t}\) particles or minijets. The study of a slight correlation shape change requires the more data statistics and left for the future study.

