### Systematic Study

In this section, possible sources of uncertainties on the transverse-momentum two-particle density ratios are considered and evaluated.

#### 6.4.1 Reconstruction Effects

Particle momentum is determined by event reconstruction software. Size of uncertainty associated with this reconstruction process is estimated by comparing two-particle density ratios of events from pure event-generating simulation and from after detector-response simulation and offline event reconstruction.

The transverse two-particle density ratios \(R_{sim}(x(p_{t1}),x(p_{t2}))\) from the simulation which reproduces realistic single-particle \(p_{t}\) distribution similar to Fig. 5.9 with null two-particle correlation signal is shown in Fig. 6.15. The upper right panels in Fig. 6.15 (i) and (ii) show \(R_{sim}\) before and after offline event reconstructions respectively. It is observed that there is no apparent correlation structure on both plots with a small change in fluctuations around 1. The upper left panels in the figures, distributions of standard deviation \(\sigma_{R_{sim}}\) or statistical error based on the number of pairs in each bin are presented. The relative variance and pair entry in each bin for CI two-particle density ratio, for example, is expressed as \(\frac{\sigma_{R_{CI}}^{2}}{R_{CI}^{2}}=(\frac{1}{N_{sib}}+\frac{1}{N_{mix}})\) where \(N_{sib}\) and \(N_{mix}\) indicate the number of sibling and mixed CI pairs in the bin, and thus the statistical error changes bin-by-bin as the pair entry vary. Difference in the statistical error distributions of (i) and (ii) implies that less number of particlescontribute to (ii) which causes the increased fluctuations on reconstructed pair density ratios. But it confirms that it does not affect correlation structure in two-particle density ratio spaces. The lower plots represent corresponding bin-number distributions of \(\sigma_{R_{sim}}\) (left-hand panels) and \(R_{sim}\) (right-hand panels), solid lines indicate gaussian functions with the maximum (blue) and minimum (red) bin variances \(\sigma_{R_{sim}}\).

The maximum standard error in real data before normalization are \(1.2\times 10^{-4}\), \(1.0\times 10^{-4}\), \(2.8\times 10^{-4}\) and \(8.5\times 10^{-4}\) from most-central to peripheral centrality classes respectively at the lower left or upper right corner of the

