#### 6.4.5 Background Contributions

There are systematic uncertainties from background contributions in a data and these contributions in the momentum acceptance under study are extensively studied for single-particle spectra analysis [68]. The main sources of background are electron background from photon conversion (Ex. \(\pi^{0}\) (decay) \(\Rightarrow\gamma+\gamma\) ; \(\gamma+N\)(detector material) \(\Rightarrow e^{+}+e^{-}\)), decay products (Ex. \(\rho^{0}\), \(\omega\), \(K_{s}^{0}\)\(\Rightarrow\pi^{+}+\pi^{-}\)) or the secondary particles produced by the interaction with detector material (Ex. \(\pi\) + \(N\)(detector material) \(\Rightarrow\)\(p+\pi\)).

The contribution from these backgrounds in the results of two-particle correlation analysis are estimated with Monte Carlo simulation using the estimated background to primary particle ratio in single-particle spectra [69]. The electron background to true primary ratio in year-1 data is small (\(<1\%\)) for the \(p_{t}\) region larger than 0.4 GeV/c, and \(\sim 10\%\) at \(p_{t}\) = 0.2 GeV/c which decreases exponentially with increasing \(p_{t}\). Thus the effect from electron contamination due to photon conversions mainly contributes at low \(p_{t}\) region. The hadronic background aggregates \(\sim\)7% of the true primary particles for the \(p_{t}\) range from 0.4 to 2 GeV/c, with a small increase to about 10% at the minimum \(p_{t}\) value of 0.15 GeV/c for this analysis.

The estimation of background correlation contribution in two-particle density ratios require the correlation models among background secondaries and between parent and secondaries. With known single-particle background-particle to primary-particle ratio \(A\), the contribution to the two-particle spacesare estimated from the background-pair to primary-pair ratio \(A^{2}\) and background-primary-pairs \(2A\).

The \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs emerge at very small opening angles (\(<1^{\circ}\)) [80] which are partially removed by pair-merging cuts of average pair separation and the small-momentum-scale correlation correction of pair \(p_{t}\) differences. The transverse momentum correlation among background- pairs and background-primary pairs are modeled by Bethe-Heitler differential cross section [81, 80]. The contribution from correlation among \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs are negligible in the two-particle transverse momentum space because of the smallness of \(e^{+}e^{-}\) to primary-pair ratio which is less than 1% at the maximum. The contribution from the correlation between primary-parent and background-daughter particles is also negligible in two-particle transverse momentum space because of small correlation among primary particles and between primary \(\pi^{0}\), for example, to the observed electrons in low \(p_{t}\) range. These expectations are also confirmed by the Monte Carlo simulation [81]. The knock-out protons from the beam pipe and other detector material could contribute to two-particle correlation for \((+,+)\) charged-pair but not \((-,-)\) pairs. Thus the negligible difference between LS two-particle correlation of different charges (Sec. 6.4.2) indicate that the proton knock-out contamination is negligible for present purpose.

The main contribution are expected from the transverse momentum correlations among hadronic secondary decay products. Similar to the consideration of electron contamination, the contribution from correlation between daughters is negligible due to the relatively small number of such pairs in the sibling pair sample. If the decay particles are completely uncorrelated with the other primaries, the contribution from parent and 7% daughter particles introduces dilution of the correlation signal by \((1-0.07)^{2}\). On the other hand, if the contaminants remain fully correlated with the primaries, then adding in this 7% background has no effect on the correlation signal. Because of the uncertainly associated with unknown parent-daughter correlation in the hadronic background, we assign a half of difference between these two extreme cases as the systematic error. For two-particle correlations this procedure leads to a 16% error \(x(p_{t})<0.4\) and 7% systematic uncertainty elsewhere. Based on the spectra analysis, we assume the same contamination fraction for all centralities.

The contribution from resonance decays (\(\rho^{0}\), \(\omega\), \(\eta\)) are estimated by Monte Carlo simulation [82] and presented in Fig. 6.23. It is shown that the effect, the positive correlation at \(|x_{\Delta}|>0.8\) and negative contribution \(|x_{\Sigma}|<1.2\), have a tendency to decrease the large-scale saddle-shaped structure.

