### Summary

The transverse-momentum two-particle correlation structure through the two-particle number-density ratio analysis of Au-Au collisions are studied for the first time at RHIC energy of \(\sqrt{s_{\,NN}}=130\) GeV. We have observed the major correlation structures which is not anticipated by existing theoretical models, in the CI two-particle transverse momentum space; (1) the large-momentum-scale saddle-shaped structure and (2) the high-\(x_{\,\Sigma}\), \(x_{\Delta}\) independent components (3) small-momentum-scale correlations (the Bose-Einstein and Coulomb correlations). The last is eliminated by cuts and the first two are the main subjects of interest in this study.

The observation of saddle-shaped large-momentum scale correlation structure is qualitatively and quantitatively described by the model based on two-particle transverse-momentum distributions which are in turn represented by two-point temperature distributions (Sec. 4.1). The residual high-\(x_{\,\Sigma}\) component of correlation structure from the two-dimensional temperature model fit have approximate independence of \(x_{\Delta}\) and its projection onto \(x_{\Sigma}\) diagonal are obtained. Projected residuals are fitted with the model function inspired by semi-hard scattering particle production in p-p collisions.

The overall correlation structure can be interpreted as the result of initial-state semi-hard parton scattering and subsequent fragmentation (mini-jet) in which partons are strongly modified by in-medium dissipation in the more central Au-Au collisions, leading to a complex temperature/velocitystructure in the pre-hadronic medium which is reflected in the observed two-particle \(p_{t}\) correlations revealed by this work.

The scale dependence of those correlation structures are also observed, however for the study of the correlation-scale in axial momentum spaces, the more investigation of soft +hard correlation components with larger event sample statistics are desired for the future study. Also, the particle identification in the studied detector acceptance is anticipated to step into the local and global temperature fluctuation for the future study.

The charge dependent part of correlation structure exhibits the approximate independence of \(x_{\Delta}\) direction and the one-dimensional projection onto \(x_{\Sigma}\) diagonal is constructed. Correlation structures in the CD transverse two-particle density ratios show a small centrality dependence in amplitude and a slight shape changes. These CI and CD two-particle correlation observations are consistent with the behaviors of event-by-event \(<p_{t}>\) fluctuation analysis.

## Chapter 7 Charge-Dependent Axial-Momentum Correlations

Measurements of charge-dependent (CD) two-particle axial momentum joint autocorrelations in Au-Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=\)130 GeV are presented in this chapter. Joint autocorrelations are formed for axial-momentum difference variables, pseudorapidity difference \(\eta_{\Delta}\equiv\eta_{1}-\eta_{2}\) and azimuth difference \(\phi_{\Delta}\equiv\phi_{1}-\phi_{2}\). Study of correlations and fluctuations has access to the dynamics in the early stages of heavy ion collisions and the color medium produced in the collisions. Specifically, in-medium modification of color-string fragmentation and hard partonic scattering in heavy ion collisions affect large-momentum-scale correlations. String fragmentation models account for axial (\(\eta\), \(\phi\)) correlations in high-energy p-p collisions in terms of local conservation of transverse momentum and net charge (Sec. 4.2) and thus produce CD correlations. Charge-dependent part of correlation structures are obtained by subtracting unlike-sign (US) joint autocorrelation from like-sign (LS). In this chapter, we consider charge-dependent or isovector aspects of correlations related to charge conservation at hadronization.

Events, tracks and pairs used in this analysis are selected according toSec. 5.1 - 5.3, and associated systematic uncertainties are considered following the result (Sec. 7.3). In this study, total of 340k events (217k central and 123k minimum-bias triggered event samples) are analyzed and divided into four centrality bins defined in Sec. 5.4 according to event multiplicities. The small-momentum-scale correlations in CD axial-momentum two-particle space are corrected as described in Sec. 5.7 unless noted.

### Two-Particle Density Ratio

Like-sign (LS) and unlike-sign (US) two-particle density ratios of axial momentum \((8\overline{\mathrm{N}}-1)(R(\eta_{1},\eta_{2})-1)\) and \((8\overline{\mathrm{N}}-1)(R(\phi_{1},\phi_{2})-1)\) for four centrality classes without small-momentum-correlation correction are shown in Fig. 7.1. In pseudorapidity two-particle space, a dominant large-scale positive correlations for US pairs (Fig. 7.1 (i)) and a small correlation structures for LS pairs (Fig. 7.1 (ii)) besides small-momentum-scale correlation near \(\eta_{\Delta}\sim 0\) diagonal are observed. Observed correlation structures resemble pseudorapidity correlations in p-p collisions and are suggestive of charge-ordering in color-string fragmentation. In azimuthal two-particle space, a quadrupole influential structure for both LS and US pairs often characterized by the second-harmonic coefficient \(v_{2}\) arising from an anisotropy of initial collision geometry (Sec. 4.3) are observed. In addition to the \(v_{2}\) component, the large-scale structure similar to that is observed in pseudorapidity space, a strong positive peak in the same-side (Fig. 7.1 (iii)) and small anti-correlation for the away-side pairs (Fig. 7.1 (iv)) are also found.

