A strong negative peaked structure of which amplitude and shape significantly change with collision centrality is observed. In both pseudorapidity and azimuth difference directions the correlation length change with centrality. Different centrality developments in each direction are clearly observed by contrasting one-dimensional \(\eta_{\Delta}\), \(\phi_{\Delta}\) autocorrelation projections obtained by taking average of joint autocorrelation over the other difference variables. Projections from different averaged ranges are plotted in Fig. 7.3. Projections from partial averages over \(|\eta_{\Delta}|<1.5\) onto \(\phi_{\Delta}\)-axis (black points) and \(\eta_{\Delta}\) projections from region \(|\phi_{\Delta}|<\pi/2\) (blue points) are shown in Fig. 7.3 (i), and full range average projection onto \(\phi_{\Delta}\)-axis (\(|\eta_{\Delta}|<2.6\)) and onto \(\eta_{\Delta}\)-axis (\(|\phi_{\Delta}|<\pi\)) are plotted in Fig. 7.3 (ii). The amplitude of two-dimensional central peaks are reduced in 1D projections by the averaging procedure. The major two-dimensional negative peak are broader and elliptical for peripheral collisions, transitioning smoothly to a narrower and deeper peak nearly symmetric on (\(\eta_{\Delta}\),\(\phi_{\Delta}\)) for central collisions.

