### Systematic Study

The uncertainty associated the two-particle axial momentum correlation constructions are considered in this section similar to that for transverse-momentum space (Sec. 6.4).

#### Reconstruction Effect

Axial-momentum variables of particles are reconstructed by tracking space-time positions of reconstructed hits obtained from ADC values measured by the STAR TPC. The effects arising from the tracking procedure are estimated by comparing two-particle ratios of simulated signals before and after detector response calculations.

In Fig. 7.6, the left-side panels (a) present joint autocorrelation from simulated events which passed through the detector response simulations and the offline tracking. The middle plots (b) show the results from input simulation data without the detector response simulation for CI (upper panels) and CD (lower panels) two-particle density ratios. In the simulated model, only CI correlation of an equal LS and US correlation is included. The absence of correlation structures in the difference distribution in the right-hand panels (c) obtained by subtracting two-dimensional distribution shown in the middle panel from the left-hand panel, indicate that the axial momentum variables are appropriately reconstructed and do not introduce the artificial effects on the axial two-particle momentum spaces. Also no observed CD correlation structures in the lower panels confirms that no pair-charge dependentoffline-tracking effect are introduced. Bin content histograms of the difference distribution in the right-hand panels are shown at the bottom (iii, iv) of which variances agree within statistical error and the effect are considered to be negligible for current study.

The statistical error distributions in the CI and CD joint autocorrelation distributions based on pair entries are shown in Fig. 7.7. In each two-particle spaces, the statistical error is approximately constant along \(\eta_{\Sigma}\), \(\phi_{\Sigma}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\) directions but quadratically increase with \(\left|\eta_{\Delta}\right|\). The standard errors on the CD and CI spaces differ by a factor of two except for a small difference variable region due to different small-momentum-scale correlation cut parameters (Sec. 5.7).

