#### Background Contributions

The contribution from non-primary background particles to the two-particle axial momentum correlations are considered following the discussion given for the transverse momentum correlations (Sec. 6.4.5) The \(e^{+}e^{-}\) pairs from photon conversion emerge at very small opening angles of less than \(1^{\circ}\)) [80] and these are partially removed by pair-merging cuts of average pair separation and the small-momentum-scale correlation correction of pair \(p_{t}\) differences. We avoid the effect from the electron background from photon conversion by excluding the central single bin of axial joint autocorrelations defined by \(|\eta_{\Delta}|<0.1\) and \(|\phi_{\Delta}|<0.1\) from model fits. The knock-out protons from the beam pipe and other detector material could contribute to two-particle correlation for \((+,+)\) charged-pair but not \((-,-)\) pairs. Thus the negligible difference between LS two-particle correlation of different charges (Sec. 7.3.2) indicate that the proton knock-out contamination is negligible in this study. Thus the main contribution from background non-primary particles (Sec. 6.4.5) to the study of two-particle axial-momentum correlations are the hadronic secondary decay products.

Because the parent primary and daughter, and two daughters correlation in the axial momentum space is unknown, the contribution from hadronic background in the axial momentum space is calculated for two extreme cases of 100% correlation, _i.e._ the daughters are in the same direction as the parent, and 0% correlation, _i.e._ the directions of daughters are independent of parent directions. Following the similar consideration as in Sec. 6.4.5 for the uncertainty associated with unknown parent-daughter correlation in the hadronic background, the uncertainty in the two-particle axial momentum space is assigned to be \(\sim 7\%\) for the dilution correction.

The contribution from resonance decays (\(\rho^{0}\), \(\omega\), \(\eta\)) are estimated by Monte Carlo simulation [82] and presented in Fig. 7.16. It is estimated that the effect exists in US pairs as the negative correlation for \(|\phi_{\Delta}|<0.5\) rad,

