### Summary

The first measurement of charge-dependent (CD) joint autocorrelations on difference variables \(\eta_{\Delta}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\) for Au-Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{NN}}=130\) GeV are presented. We observed a strong CD correlation over the large momentum difference scale comparable to TPC acceptance and its development with collision centrality.

The structure are dominated by a two-dimensional negative peak. The peak is broader and elliptical for peripheral collisions, and narrower nearly symmetric peak on \(\eta_{\Delta}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\) is observed for central collisions. The centrality dependence of the strong peak exhibits a large amplitude increase, significant width reduction and transition to approximately equal width on \(\eta_{\Delta}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\) direction. The CD correlation for the peripheral Au-Au events, increased \(\phi_{\Delta}\) width and the emergence of a single gaussian peak on \(\eta_{\Delta}\), suggests an asymptotic trend toward p-p collisions.

The CD correlation structures in Au-Au collisions are consistent with local charge conservation or canonical suppression of net charge fluctuations. However the significant centrality dependence suggests that the charge-ordering geometry is evolving from 1D color-string fragmentation observed in p-p collisions to exponentially attenuated 2D charge-ordered emission from a hadron-opaque medium formed in central Au-Au collisions. Charge-dependent autocorrelations provide unique access to the evolution of the hadron production geometry from spatially expanding bulk medium produced in the heavy ion collisions.

