## Chapter 9 Conclusion

I presented in this dissertation the first measurements of surviving correlation structures among unidentified charged hadron distributions produced in collisions of gold nuclei at \(\sqrt{s\,_{NN}}=130\) GeV measured by the STAR detector at RHIC. An abundant correlation structures over a large momentum scale comparable to the STAR TPC acceptance is observed in transverse momentum \(p_{t}\) range \(0.15\leq p_{t}\leq 2.0\) GeV/c, pseudorapidity range \(-1.3\leq\eta\leq 1.3\), and full azimuth angle acceptance \(-\pi\leq\phi\leq\pi\). Pair-charge dependent (CD) and independent (CI) part of correlation structures are measured by means of two-particle number density ratios in transverse and axial two-particle momentum spaces.

In the CI two-particle transverse momentum number density ratios of transformed-\(p_{t}\) variable (\(x\,(p_{t})\)), we find significant large-momentum-scale saddle shaped structures composed of a concave upward and downward along sum- and difference-axis respectively. The structures (\(x\,(p_{t1})+x\,(p_{t2})\leq 1.6\)) are qualitatively and quantitatively described by a two-dimensional Levy distribution inspired by the temperature fluctuation model. The residual structure at large \(x\)-sum exhibits an approximate independence of difference-axis, andis described by a gaussian model function of the transverse rapidity consistent with the hard scattering particle distribution in p-p collisions. The observed correlation structures are consistent with a scenario in which the particles originated from initial-state hard parton scattering and their associated fragmentation to hadrons (mini-jet) are modified by in-medium dissipation in the more central Au-Au collisions. These dynamics lead to a complex temperature/velocity structure in the pre-hadronic medium and the complex structure is reflected in the observed final-state two-particle \(p_{t}\) correlations revealed by this work.

In two-particle axial momentum joint autocorrelations, we observed large-momentum scale CD negatively peaked structure and CI correlations associated with elliptic flow and positively peaked structure corresponding to mini-jets. Both CD and CI correlations show the significant shape and amplitude change with increasing collision centrality.

The CD correlation in the two-particle axial momentum space observed in p-p collisions are phenomenologically described by the color-string fragmentation model in terms of local conservation of transverse momentum and net charge. The correlation structures observed in Au-Au collisions are compared with p-p axial momentum correlations, and we observe the CD correlation corresponding to the string fragmentation model to be markedly different in detail. In central Au-Au collisions, a large amplitude two-dimensional peak, approximately exponential in form, dominates the correlation structures with similar width on \(\eta_{\Delta}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\) much reduced from p-p collisions. The centrality dependence of the correlation toward the most-peripheral centrality class suggests an asymptotic trend toward p-p collisions as increased \(\phi_{\Delta}\) width and the emergence of a single gaussian peak on \(\eta_{\Delta}\). The CD correlation observation is consistent with evolution of hadron emission geometry with increasing centrality from one-dimensional fragmentation of color strings to the two-dimensional bulk hadronization emitted from an opaque color medium.

The CI axial momentum two-particle correlation structures are dominated by a quadrupole component on \(\phi_{\Delta}\) and a strong positive peak at same-side azimuth difference dependent both on \(\phi_{\Delta}\) and \(\eta_{\Delta}\). The latter structure is extensively studied in this analysis. We observed a strong centrality development of the same-side correlation structures. In peripheral events, the structure exhibits a two-dimensional gaussian peak similar to observed in p-p collisions associated with minijet production with comparable width on \(\eta_{\Delta}\) and \(\phi_{\Delta}\). In central collisions the structure is still dominated by a 2D gaussian peak but widths on the two difference variables (\(\phi_{\Delta}\) and \(\eta_{\Delta}\)) differ by a factor 3. These resulted from a quite different centrality development of the correlation along \(\phi_{\Delta}\) and \(\eta_{\Delta}\) axis. This observation is consistent with the picture that the fragmentation of partons (minijet production) is the main contributor to particle production in the low-\(p_{t}\) region and the structure modification with centrality suggests a strong coupling of partons to a longitudinally-expanding color dissipative medium formed in Au-Au collisions.

We find that all correlation structures appeared on the different momentum spaces, and that the pair charge-combinations are consistent with a picture that parton and color-string fragmentation processes are largely contributing in the particle production in \(p_{t}<2.0\) GeV/c and that these processes are strongly coupled to a opaque or dissipative medium created in the relativistic heavy ion collisions. The medium has characteristic properties in the response to the impetus correlations. The centrality development of the correlation structures thus leads to detailed understanding of the development of the medium and heavy ion dynamics.

We confirmed by this study that the two-particle correlation structures produced in relativistic heavy ion collisions contain significant information about the color medium produced in collisions. Specifically, in-medium modification of color-string fragmentation and hard partonic scattering in heavy ion collisions are reflected in the large-momentum-scale correlation structures. This highly motivates future studies of correlation structures in heavy ion collisions, such as high-statistic correlation analysis for studies of finer centralities and acceptance dependencies and more specific correlation studies by particle identifications. Such studies should help to distinguish the particle velocity fluctuations from temperature fluctuations and lead to a further understanding about medium developments and its response. These analysis will provide critical information for understanding of color deconfined matter and descriptions of non-perturbative QCD.

